WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.981
The checklist.

2
00:00:01.261 --> 00:00:03.843
Compare configurations, not brand names.

3
00:00:04.123 --> 00:00:14.555
Fix the task, data, graders, and conditions; give every candidate the same tuning budget on dev data; score once on held-out data, paired against the incumbent.

4
00:00:14.835 --> 00:00:18.419
Match or sweep reasoning effort and keep harness settings equal.

5
00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:28.725
Price per successful task from measured usage, including thinking tokens, retries, and the cost of failures; treat latency as a constraint; draw the frontier.

6
00:00:29.005 --> 00:00:39.501
Read public benchmarks for contamination, saturation, implementation sensitivity, selective reporting, and who ran them, and use them only to build a shortlist.

7
00:00:39.781 --> 00:00:44.560
Write the decision rule before you look, and roll out with a canary and a rollback plan.

8
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:51.112
The lab this week gives you the analysis half of this as code you can reuse in every future comparison.

9
00:00:51.392 --> 00:00:56.597
One last habit, the one that makes the rest cheap: keep the bake-off suite after the decision.

10
00:00:56.877 --> 00:01:02.403
Store the frozen configurations, the test set, the results, and the decision record together.

11
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:10.128
When the next model is released, rerunning is an afternoon’s work, and the decision record tells you exactly which rule to apply.

12
00:01:10.408 --> 00:01:15.464
Teams that skip this have the same argument every quarter, with less evidence each time.
